    "Depending on whether you are looking for neutral, positive, or descriptive terms, here are ten words used to describe body shapes, categorized by their typical usage:\n\n### General & Neutral\n1. **Athletic** – Firm, toned, and usually suggests someone who exercises regularly.\n2. **Slender** – Thin in an attractive or graceful way.\n3. **Curvy** – Having a shapely figure with defined curves (often used for hourglass shapes).\n4. **Stocky** – Broad and sturdy; usually describes someone who is short but strong.\n5. **Petite** – Small and slender (typically used for women who are shorter than average).\n\n### Descriptive & Specific\n6. **Pear-shaped** – Wider at the hips than at the shoulders.\n7. **Apple-shaped** – Carrying more weight around the middle/stomach area.\n8. **Lanky** – Tall and thin, often suggesting a bit of awkwardness in movement.\n9. **Voluptuous** – Full-figured and shapely (usually used as a positive descriptor).\n10. **Lean** – Thin but with visible muscle definition; low body fat.",
    "Depending on whether you are describing someone in a medical, artistic, or casual way, here are ten common words used to describe body shape:\n\n### General/Neutral Terms\n1. **Athletic** – Firm and toned; usually implies someone who exercises regularly.\n2. **Slender** – Gracefully thin; a positive way to describe a lean frame.\n3. **Curvy** – Having a shapely figure with defined curves (often used for hourglass shapes).\n4. **Stocky** – Broad and sturdy; usually describes someone who is short but strong.\n5. **Petite** – Small and slender (typically used for women who are shorter than average).\n\n### Descriptive/Specific Terms\n6. **Lanky** – Tall and thin, often implying a bit of awkwardness in movement.\n7. **Plump** – Slightly overweight in a soft, rounded way.\n8. **Muscular** – Having well-defined muscles and a powerful build.\n9. **Pear-shaped** – Wider at the hips than at the shoulders.\n10. **Willowy** – Tall, slender, and graceful (like a willow tree).",
    "Depending on whether you are describing someone in a medical, artistic, or casual way, the words you choose will change. Here are ten words describing body shape, categorized by their tone:\n\n### Neutral/Descriptive\n1. **Athletic** – Firm and toned, usually implying someone who exercises regularly.\n2. **Slender** – Thin in an attractive or graceful way.\n3. **Curvy** – Having a shaped figure with prominent curves (often used for hourglass shapes).\n4. **Stocky** – Broad and sturdy; usually describes someone who is short but strong.\n5. **Petite** – Small and slender (typically used for women).\n\n### Broad/Larger\n6. **Plump** – Slightly overweight in a soft, rounded way (often used politely).\n7. **Husky** – Big and strong (often used for men or boys).\n8. **Voluptuous** – Full-figured and shapely (typically used for women).\n\n### Lean/Thin\n9. **Lanky** – Tall and thin, often implying a bit of awkwardness in movement.\n10. **Wiry** – Thin but strong and muscular, like a marathon runner."
